WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
GGUF.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:07.300
The file format that lets language models run on laptops, Macs and edge boxes.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.233
GGUF is the file format of the llama dot cpp ecosystem.

4
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:16.733
Everything the runtime needs is packed into one file.

5
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:21.200
Metadata first: the architecture, context length and RoPE settings.

6
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.767
Then the tokenizer.

7
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:27.500
And finally the weight tensors, with quantization baked in at a named level.

8
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.867
The level names encode the trade-off.

9
00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.700
Q eight is near lossless, but the biggest.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.933
Q four K M is the community's sweet spot.

11
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.272
Q two K is the desperate option.

12
00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:43.667
A bigger number means more bits, more gigabytes, and better fidelity.

13
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:46.467
Here's what that means in size.

14
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:52.000
The four bit version of an eight billion parameter model is about four point nine gigabytes.

15
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.767
The eight bit version is about eight and a half.

16
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:58.767
In practice, you meet GGUF through Ollama.

17
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.233
You choose the quantization level right in the name you pull.

18
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:06.533
Then run the same prompt on both, and compare answer quality and speed.

19
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.033
Here's why this matters.

20
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:12.967
You demo Mission Control at a meetup with unreliable Wi-Fi.

21
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:19.433
The whole stack runs locally: Chroma on disk, and a Q four K M model in Ollama on your MacBook.

22
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:25.067
One file pulled the night before, and the demo survives even if the network dies mid talk.

23
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:27.302
Let's recap.

24
00:01:27.302 --> 00:01:31.100
GGUF is one portable file for llama dot cpp.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.800
It holds metadata, tokenizer and quantized weights.

26
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.267
Level names encode the size versus quality trade.

27
00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:41.572
And Q four K M is the everyday default.

28
00:01:41.572 --> 00:01:43.267
Next up: BitsAndBytes.
